{
  "id": "sdf-mesh-visibility",
  "title": "Rasterized meshes and SDF surfaces resolve to the nearer one, and a mesh against the sky reaches the resolve",
  "binding": "The positive leg boots an offscreen 256x144 world of three static placements in front of a fixed camera, each a prototype whose creation and inline mesh sit together: on the left a quad in front of its block, in the centre a wider quad behind its block, and on the right a quad against the sky whose only shape is out of view. It selects the visibility debug view, which colors each pixel by the kind of its visibility record, and captures a frame. Each region's kind is the analytic oracle's (SdfMeshCanaryOracleLawTests): the fixed-point raycast of the stamped program, bounded by the far distance and never by a mesh, beside exact intersections with the mesh draws' triangles, for every slot of the layout at its render extent and reconstructed wherever the render scale is reduced, and no region reaches within two pixels of an SDF silhouette, which the engine's march widens by accepting a surface within a pixel footprint of the ray. The quad in front of the left block and the wider quad above the centre block read as mesh, both blocks read as SDF where they are nearer than the mesh, the quad against the sky reads as mesh, and the corner reads as background. It then captures the same scene in a split layout, a half-size slot and a quarter-size one of the same camera, where each slot resolves its own quads and blocks; at the half render scale tier, where the reconstructed interiors still resolve; and after row edits park the three placements and bring in three scenes: a frame whose opening shows a mesh with nothing behind it (the mesh inside the opening, background below it), a mesh lying in a block's front face (an equal-depth tie, which the SDF wins because its march accepts the face within its threshold, strictly nearer than the mesh), and a sphere in front of a wider mesh (the mesh beside and above the sphere's silhouette, the sphere over it). Last, a mesh floor running from behind the camera, which the rasterizer clips at the near plane, and a block in front of a mesh about two hundred units away, past the SDF far distance, all read in one frame. The discriminating leg boots the same world with one mesh left, out of view, so its frames still draw a mesh and run the hit passes over the whole view (a frame drawing none shows the sky outside the tiles the beam could not prove empty), and every mesh observation turns red while the SDF, background and tie observations still hold.",
  "bootShape": "offscreen",
  "backends": ["vulkan", "directx"],
  "requirements": ["gpu"],
  "timeoutSeconds": 60,
